** Compiling

1. Press =C-c C-c=
2. Press =RET= for default command (LaTeX)
3. Repeat =C-c C-c= and select =View= to open PDF

Documents compile with LuaLaTeX automatically. A dedicated =LuaLaTeX= command is also available in the command list.

** PDF Synctex

Click in PDF → jumps to source location.
=C-c C-v= from source → jumps to PDF location.

** Unlimited Sectioning

| Level | Command |
|-------|---------|
| 1 | =\\section{}= |
| 2 | =\\subsection{}= |
| 3 | =\\subsubsection{}= |
| 4 | =\\paragraph{}= |
| 5 | =\\subparagraph{}= |
| 6 | =\\subsubparagraph{}= |
| 7 | =\\subsubsubparagraph{}= |
| 8 | =\\subsubsubsubparagraph{}= |
| 9 | =\\subsubsubsubsubparagraph{}= |
| 10+ | (continue pattern) |

** Unlimited List Nesting

Standard LaTeX limits lists to 4 levels. With this config, nest up to 20 levels:

#+begin_src latex
\\begin{itemize}
  \\item Level 1
  \\begin{itemize}
    \\item Level 2
    \\begin{itemize}
      \\item Level 3
      % ... continue to level 20
    \\end{itemize}
  \\end{itemize}
\\end{itemize}
#+end_src

** Quick Preamble Insertion

| Command | Action |
|---------|--------|
| =M-x my/insert-hebrew-preamble= | Insert Babel Hebrew preamble |
| =M-x my/insert-polyglossia-preamble= | Insert Polyglossia preamble |
| =M-x my/insert-unlimited-structure= | Insert only unlimited structure |

Or use =C-c P= for the document systems hydra.

* Nested Footnotes in LaTeX

The =bigfoot= package provides multiple footnote levels. Each level appears in its own *separate block* at the bottom of the page.

** Method 1: Simple Footnotes (Non-Nested)

Use different footnote commands directly from body text:

#+begin_src latex
Main text.\\footnote{First level footnote.}

More text.\\footnoteB{Second level, appears in separate block.}
#+end_src

*Result at page bottom:*

#+begin_example
─────────────────────────────
¹ First level footnote.

─────────────────────────────
¹ Second level, appears in separate block.
#+end_example

** Method 2: True Nesting (Footnote Within Footnote)

To place a footnote *inside* another footnote, use =\\footnotemarkX= + =\\footnotetextX{}=:

#+begin_src latex
Main text.\\footnote{%
  This is the first-level footnote. It discusses a concept\\footnotemarkB{}
  that needs further explanation.
  \\footnotetextB{%
    This second-level footnote appears in its own separate block.
    It can even contain another level\\footnotemarkC{}.
    \\footnotetextC{Third level footnote, in its own block.}%
  }%
}
#+end_src

** Available Footnote Levels

| Level | Simple | Mark | Text |
|-------|--------|------|------|
| 1 | =\\footnote{}= | — | — |
| 2 | =\\footnoteB{}= | =\\footnotemarkB= | =\\footnotetextB{}= |
| 3 | =\\footnoteC{}= | =\\footnotemarkC= | =\\footnotetextC{}= |
| 4 | =\\footnoteD{}= | =\\footnotemarkD= | =\\footnotetextD{}= |
| 5 | =\\footnoteE{}= | =\\footnotemarkE= | =\\footnotetextE{}= |
| 6 | =\\footnoteF{}= | =\\footnotemarkF= | =\\footnotetextF{}= |
| 7 | =\\footnoteG{}= | =\\footnotemarkG= | =\\footnotetextG{}= |
| 8 | =\\footnoteH{}= | =\\footnotemarkH= | =\\footnotetextH{}= |
| 9 | =\\footnoteI{}= | =\\footnotemarkI= | =\\footnotetextI{}= |
| 10 | =\\footnoteJ{}= | =\\footnotemarkJ= | =\\footnotetextJ{}= |

** Keybindings

*** Simple Footnotes (=C-c f <number>=)

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c f 1= | Insert =\\footnote{}= |
| =C-c f 2= | Insert =\\footnoteB{}= |
| =C-c f 3= | Insert =\\footnoteC{}= |
| =C-c f 4= | Insert =\\footnoteD{}= |
| =C-c f 5= | Insert =\\footnoteE{}= |
| =C-c f 6= | Insert =\\footnoteF{}= |
| =C-c f 7= | Insert =\\footnoteG{}= |
| =C-c f 8= | Insert =\\footnoteH{}= |
| =C-c f 9= | Insert =\\footnoteI{}= |
| =C-c f 0= | Insert =\\footnoteJ{}= |

*** LaTeX Footnotes Hydra (=C-c F=)

Press =C-c F= to open the footnotes menu:

#+begin_example
LaTeX Footnotes (Simple)

1: \\footnote    2: \\footnoteB   3: \\footnoteC   4: \\footnoteD   5: \\footnoteE
6: \\footnoteF   7: \\footnoteG   8: \\footnoteH   9: \\footnoteI   0: \\footnoteJ

n: Nested template   m: → Marks menu   t: → Texts menu
q: quit
#+end_example

In the *marks* sub-menu (=m=):

#+begin_example
Footnote Marks (for nesting)

2: \\footnotemarkB   3: \\footnotemarkC   4: \\footnotemarkD   5: \\footnotemarkE
b: ← Back   q: quit
#+end_example

In the *texts* sub-menu (=t=):

#+begin_example
Footnote Texts (for nesting)

2: \\footnotetextB   3: \\footnotetextC   4: \\footnotetextD   5: \\footnotetextE
b: ← Back   q: quit
#+end_example

** Adding More Levels

If you need more than 10 levels, add to your preamble:

#+begin_src latex
\\DeclareNewFootnote{K}
\\DeclareNewFootnote{L}
#+end_src

Then use =\\footnoteK{}=, =\\footnotemarkK=, =\\footnotetextK{}=, etc.

* ConTeXt

** What is ConTeXt?

ConTeXt is a TeX-based document system with:
- Native RTL/Hebrew support (no packages needed)
- Built-in local footnotes (true hierarchical nesting)
- Excellent typography
- \"Batteries included\" philosophy

ConTeXt is included in =texlive.combined.scheme-full= (provided by =emacs.nix=).

** Creating a ConTeXt Document

Use =C-c h x= (or =C-c h n= → =context=) to create a Hebrew ConTeXt document, or create manually:

#+begin_src tex
\\mainlanguage[he]
\\setupalign[r2l]
\\definefontfamily[hebrew][rm][David CLM]
\\definefontfamily[hebrew][ss][Nachlieli CLM]
\\definefontfamily[hebrew][tt][Miriam Mono CLM]
\\setupbodyfont[hebrew]

\\starttext

\\startsection[title={מבוא}]

טקסט בעברית.

\\stopsection

\\stoptext
#+end_src

** Compiling ConTeXt

In Emacs:
1. Press =C-c C-c=
2. Type =ConTeXt= and press =RET=

Or from command line:

#+begin_src shell
context myfile.tex
#+end_src

** ConTeXt Sectioning

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| =\\startpart[title={...}]= | Part |
| =\\startchapter[title={...}]= | Chapter |
| =\\startsection[title={...}]= | Section |
| =\\startsubsection[title={...}]= | Subsection |
| =\\startsubsubsection[title={...}]= | Subsubsection |

Each must be closed with =\\stop...=

** Local Footnotes in ConTeXt

ConTeXt's killer feature: footnotes that appear at the bottom of their *parent footnote*, not at the bottom of the page.

#+begin_src tex
זהו טקסט ראשי.\\footnote{
  זוהי הערת שוליים ראשונה.
  \\startlocalfootnotes
  מונח כלשהו.\\localfootnote{
    הערה מקוננת — מופיעה בתחתית ההערה הראשונה.
    \\startlocalfootnotes
    עוד מונח.\\localfootnote{
      הערה שלישית — בתחתית ההערה השנייה.
    }
    \\placelocalfootnotes
    \\stoplocalfootnotes
  }
  \\placelocalfootnotes
  \\stoplocalfootnotes
}
#+end_src

*Result:*
- Level 1 footnote → bottom of page
- Level 2 footnote → bottom of level 1 block
- Level 3 footnote → bottom of level 2 block

Use =C-c P= then =f= to insert a local footnote structure.

* Typst

** What is Typst?

Typst is a modern document system with:
- Instant compilation (watch mode)
- Clean, readable syntax
- Native RTL support
- Easy scripting
- ~50MB vs ~4GB for full LaTeX

** Creating a Typst Document

Use =C-c h t= (or =C-c h n= → =typst=) to create a Hebrew Typst document, or create manually:

#+begin_src typst
#set text(lang: \"he\", font: \"David CLM\")
#set page(flipped: true)
#set heading(numbering: \"1.1.1\")

= מבוא

טקסט בעברית.

== תת-כותרת

עוד טקסט.
#+end_src

** Compiling Typst

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c C-c= | Compile once |
| =C-c C-w= | Watch mode (live preview) |
| =C-c C-v= | View PDF |
| =C-c t h= | Insert Hebrew preamble |
| =C-c t f= | Insert nested footnote helper |

Or from command line:

#+begin_src shell
typst compile myfile.typ      # Compile once
typst watch myfile.typ        # Watch mode (recompiles on save)
#+end_src

** Typst Syntax Basics

*** Headings

#+begin_src typst
= Level 1
== Level 2
=== Level 3
==== Level 4 (unlimited)
#+end_src

*** Text Formatting

#+begin_src typst
*bold*
_italic_
`code`
#underline[underlined]
#strike[strikethrough]
#+end_src

*** Lists

#+begin_src typst
- Unordered item
  - Nested item
    - More nesting (unlimited)

+ Ordered item
  + Nested ordered
#+end_src

*** Links

#+begin_src typst
#link(\"https://example.com\")[Link text]
#+end_src

** Basic Footnotes

#+begin_src typst
This is text#footnote[This is a footnote].
#+end_src

** Nested Footnotes in Typst

*** The Problem

By default, Typst doesn't allow =#footnote= inside another =#footnote=.

*** The Solution

Add this show rule at the top of your document:

#+begin_src typst
#show footnote.entry: it => {
  block(it)
}
#+end_src

*** Full Example

#+begin_src typst
#show footnote.entry: it => {
  block(it)
}

This is body text with a footnote.#footnote[
  This is the first-level footnote, and it contains
  another footnote inside it.#footnote[
    This is a second-level (nested) footnote! You can even
    go deeper.#footnote[
      Third level nested footnote.
    ]
  ]
]
#+end_src

*Result at page bottom:*

#+begin_example
──────────────────────────────────────
¹ This is the first-level footnote, and it contains
  another footnote inside it.²
² This is a second-level (nested) footnote! You can even
  go deeper.³
³ Third level nested footnote.
#+end_example

*** Hebrew Example

#+begin_src typst
#set text(lang: \"he\", font: \"David CLM\")
#set page(flipped: true)

#show footnote.entry: it => {
  block(it)
}

= מבוא

זהו טקסט ראשי עם הערת שוליים.#footnote[
  זוהי הערת שוליים ראשונה, המכילה הערה נוספת.#footnote[
    זוהי הערת שוליים מקוננת!#footnote[
      הערת שוליים ברמה שלישית.
    ]
  ]
]
#+end_src

** Typst LSP Support

If =tinymist= is installed (provided by =emacs.nix=), Eglot starts automatically and provides:
- Syntax checking
- Autocompletion
- Hover documentation

** Typst Tree-Sitter Mode

The configuration uses =typst-ts-mode= (tree-sitter based) for enhanced syntax highlighting. It is installed via =package-vc-install= from =https://git.sr.ht/~meow_king/typst-ts-mode=.

If tree-sitter grammars are not available, a simple =typst-mode= fallback is defined automatically.

Dependencies:
- =treesit-auto= — installed from MELPA for tree-sitter grammar management

* Nested Footnotes Comparison

| Feature | LaTeX (bigfoot) | ConTeXt | Typst |
|---------|-----------------|---------|-------|
| *Setup required* | Package + declare | None (built-in) | One show rule |
| *Syntax* | =\\footnotemarkB= etc. | =\\localfootnote{}= | =#footnote[]= inside |
| *Separate visual blocks* | ✓ Yes | ✓ Yes (hierarchical) | ✗ No (sequential) |
| *Numbering* | Per-level | Per-level | Flat (all share) |
| *Max practical levels* | 10+ | Unlimited | ~3 |
| *Hierarchical display* | ✗ Parallel blocks | ✓ True nesting | ✗ Sequential |
| *Best for* | Journals, academic | Books, complex docs | Quick iteration |

*Recommendations:*
- For true hierarchical footnotes: *ConTeXt*
- For journal compatibility: *LaTeX* with bigfoot
- For fast iteration: *Typst*

* Org-Roam

** What is Org-Roam?

Org-roam is a note-taking system based on the Zettelkasten method. It creates a network of interconnected notes with:
- Bidirectional links
- Backlink tracking
- Visual graph
- Daily notes

** Directory Structure

#+begin_example
~/Documents/roam/
├── 20250115120000-note-title.org
├── 20250115130000-another-note.org
├── ...
└── daily/
    ├── 2025-01-15.org
    ├── 2025-01-16.org
    └── ...
#+end_example

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c n f= | Find or create node |
| =C-c n i= | Insert link to node |
| =C-c n l= | Toggle backlinks buffer |
| =C-c n c= | Capture new node |
| =C-c n g= | Show graph |
| =C-c n t= | Add tag to node |
| =C-c n r= | Add reference |
| =C-c n a= | Add alias |
| =C-c n d= | Go to today's daily note |
| =C-c n D= | Go to specific date |
| =C-c n h= | Find Hebrew-tagged nodes |
| =C-c n R= | Find research-tagged nodes |
| =C-c n x= | Open random node |
| =C-c R= | Org-roam hydra menu |

** Org-Roam Hydra (=C-c R=)

#+begin_example
Org-Roam

f: Find node       i: Insert node      c: Capture
l: Toggle buffer   g: Graph            t: Add tag
d: Daily today     D: Daily date       u: Roam UI
q: quit
#+end_example

** Creating Notes

1. Press =C-c n f=
2. Type a title
3. If it doesn't exist, press =RET= to create
4. Choose a template:

| Key | Template | Description |
|-----|----------|-------------|
| =d= | Default | Basic note |
| =h= | Hebrew | Note with Hebrew settings |
| =r= | Research | Research note with source fields |
| =c= | Concept | Definition, examples, related |
| =l= | Literature | Bibliographic note |
| =p= | Project | Goals, tasks, resources |

5. Write your note
6. Save with =C-x C-s=

** Linking Notes

1. In a note, press =C-c n i=
2. Search for note to link to
3. Select and press =RET=

The link syntax is:

#+begin_example
[[id:abc123][Note Title]]
#+end_example

** Viewing Backlinks

Press =C-c n l= to see all notes that link TO the current note. The backlinks buffer shows:
- Linking note title
- Context around the link

** Daily Notes

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c n d= | Go to today's daily |
| =C-c n D= | Go to specific date |

Daily note templates:

| Key | Template | Description |
|-----|----------|-------------|
| =d= | Default | Simple timestamped entries |
| =h= | Hebrew | Hebrew daily with tasks/notes sections |

** Visual Graph

Run =M-x org-roam-ui-open= to see your notes as an interactive graph in a web browser.

The graph shows:
- Notes as nodes
- Links as edges
- Tags as colors
- Hover for preview

** Integration with Citar

Literature notes can be linked to bibliography entries:
1. Create a literature note (=C-c n c l=)
2. Use =C-c b i= to insert citation
3. Notes are automatically linked via =citar-org-roam=

* PDF Tools and Annotation

** Opening PDFs

PDFs open automatically in =pdf-view-mode= with:
- Smooth scrolling
- Midnight mode (dark background)
- Synctex support for LaTeX

PDF-tools is provided by Nix and auto-configured (no need to run =pdf-tools-install= manually).

** PDF Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =n= / =p= | Next/previous page |
| =SPC= / =DEL= | Scroll down/up |
| =+= / =-= | Zoom in/out |
| =0= | Fit page to window |
| =W= | Fit width |
| =H= | Fit height |
| =g= | Go to page |
| =o= | Outline (table of contents) |
| =s= | Search |
| =C-s= | Isearch |
| =m= | Set bookmark |
| =B= | List bookmarks |

** Org-Noter Integration

Org-noter allows you to take notes linked to specific PDF locations.

*** Starting a Session

1. Open a PDF
2. Press =C-c n= (in pdf-view-mode)
3. Create or select a notes file

*** During Annotation

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =i= | Insert note at current location |
| =M-i= | Insert precise note |
| =C-M-n= / =C-M-p= | Next/previous note |
| =C-M-.= | Sync PDF to note |
| =q= | Quit org-noter |

*** Note Structure

Notes are organized hierarchically:
- Level 1: Document title
- Level 2: Page/location notes
- Timestamps link to exact PDF positions

*** Workflow

1. Open PDF in one window
2. Notes file in adjacent window
3. Navigate PDF, insert notes with =i=
4. Notes store page number and position
5. Click note → jumps to PDF location

* Bibliography Management

** Citar

Citar provides bibliography management integrated with Org and LaTeX.

** Bibliography File

Location: =~/Documents/bibliography.bib= (created automatically if missing).

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c b o= | Open bibliography entry (PDF, URL, etc.) |
| =C-c b i= | Insert citation |
| =C-c b n= | Open/create notes for entry |
| =C-c b r= | Refresh bibliography cache |

** Adding References

Edit =~/Documents/bibliography.bib= and add BibTeX entries:

#+begin_src bibtex
@article{smith2024,
  author = {Smith, John},
  title = {Article Title},
  journal = {Journal Name},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {1},
  pages = {1--10}
}

@book{jones2023,
  author = {Jones, Sarah},
  title = {Book Title},
  publisher = {Publisher Name},
  year = {2023}
}

@misc{rambam,
  author = {Maimonides},
  title = {Mishneh Torah},
  year = {1180},
  note = {Hebrew}
}
#+end_src

After editing, run =C-c b r= to refresh.

** Inserting Citations

*** In Org Mode

1. Press =C-c b i=
2. Search for entry
3. Select and press =RET=

Result:

#+begin_example
[cite:@smith2024]
#+end_example

*** In LaTeX

1. Press =C-c b i=
2. Search and select
3. Insert style (cite, parencite, textcite, etc.)

Result:

#+begin_src latex
\\cite{smith2024}
\\parencite{smith2024}
\\textcite{smith2024}
#+end_src

** Integration with Org-Roam

Literature notes are automatically linked:

1. Press =C-c n c= then =l= (literature template)
2. Or press =C-c b n= on a bibliography entry
3. Notes are created with bibliographic metadata

The note title format is: \"Author - Title\"

** LaTeX Bibliography Setup

#+begin_src latex
\\usepackage[backend=biber,style=authoryear]{biblatex}
\\addbibresource{~/Documents/bibliography.bib}

% In document:
See \\textcite{smith2024} for details.

% At end:
\\printbibliography
#+end_src

** Org Export with Citations

#+begin_example
,#+BIBLIOGRAPHY: ~/Documents/bibliography.bib

See [cite:@smith2024] for details.

,#+PRINT_BIBLIOGRAPHY:
#+end_example

* Seforim Library

A complete system for managing and searching large Hebrew text (Seforim) collections.

** Overview

| Tool | Purpose | Speed | Format Support |
|------|---------|-------|----------------|
| plocate | Find by filename | Instant (indexed) | All |
| recoll | Full-text search | Instant (indexed) | org, pdf, epub, docx, doc, odt |
| ripgrep-all | Fallback text search | Fast (live) | org, pdf, epub, docx, doc, odt |
| fd | Fallback file find | Fast (live) | All |

The system uses indexed search (plocate/recoll) as primary, with ripgrep-all/fd as fallbacks when indexes are unavailable.

** NixOS Integration

The =emacs.nix= module provides:
- All search tools (plocate, recoll, ripgrep-all, fd)
- Document converters (pandoc, antiword, catdoc, unzip, calibre) for recoll and rga
- Plocate service with hourly indexing (=services.locate=)
- Recoll configuration (=~/.recoll/recoll.conf=, managed by NixOS)
- Systemd timer for periodic recoll indexing
- User membership in =plocate= group
- Seforim directory creation

** Directory Structure

#+begin_example
~/Documents/seforim/
├── Torah/
│   ├── Bereishit.org
│   ├── Shemot.org
│   └── ...
├── Neviim/
│   └── ...
├── Ketuvim/
│   └── ...
├── Mishnah/
│   └── ...
├── Bavli/
│   ├── Berakhot.org
│   └── ...
├── Yerushalmi/
│   └── ...
├── Rambam/
│   └── ...
└── ...
#+end_example

** Seforim Hydra (=C-c S=)

#+begin_example
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     SEFORIM                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ FIND FILE          │ SEARCH CONTENT                 │
│  f find (plocate)  │  s search (recoll)             │
│  F find (fuzzy)    │  r search (ripgrep-all)        │
│                    │  c search current dir           │
│                    │  S search choose dir            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ NAVIGATE           │ MANAGE                         │
│  o outline         │  I reindex all                  │
│  t toggle sidebar  │  ? status                      │
│  b browse root     │                                │
│  B browse current  │                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                     q quit
#+end_example

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c S= | Open Seforim hydra |
| =C-c S f= | Find file (plocate, indexed) |
| =C-c S F= | Find file (fuzzy, interactive) |
| =C-c S s= | Search text (recoll, indexed) |
| =C-c S r= | Search text (ripgrep-all, fallback) |
| =C-c S c= | Search in current directory |
| =C-c S S= | Choose directory then search |
| =C-c S o= | Jump to heading (outline) |
| =C-c S t= | Toggle Dirvish sidebar |
| =C-c S b= | Browse library root |
| =C-c S B= | Browse current file's directory |
| =C-c S I= | Reindex all (plocate + recoll) |
| =C-c S ?= | Show status of tools and indexes |

** Finding a Sefer

*** By Filename (Indexed)

1. Press =C-c S f=
2. Type fragments: =bav shab= matches =Bavli/Shabbat.org=
3. Select and press =RET=

Uses plocate (instant, indexed). Falls back to fd if plocate unavailable.

*** By Filename (Fuzzy)

1. Press =C-c S F=
2. Type interactively with live filtering
3. Uses =consult-fd= for fuzzy matching

** Searching Content

*** Full-Text Search (Indexed)

1. Press =C-c S s=
2. Type search term (Hebrew or English): =אברהם=
3. Results show with context
4. Select to open file at match

Uses recoll (instant, indexed). Searches inside PDFs, EPUB, DOCX, and ODT files as well as plain text.

*** Ripgrep-All Search (Fallback)

1. Press =C-c S r=
2. Type search term
3. Uses =consult-ripgrep= with =rga= backend for live preview

Also supports searching inside PDF, EPUB, DOCX, and other binary formats.

*** Directory-Scoped Search

| Command | Scope |
|---------|-------|
| =C-c S c= | Current file's directory |
| =C-c S S= | Choose directory interactively |

** Navigating Within a Sefer

1. Open any =.org= sefer file
2. Press =C-c S o= (or =M-x consult-outline=)
3. Jump to:
   - Perakim (chapters)
   - Daf numbers (=2a=, =2b=, etc.)
   - Simanim
   - Halachot

** Browsing the Hierarchy

*** Dirvish File Browser

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c S b= | Open Dirvish at library root |
| =C-c S B= | Open Dirvish at current directory |
| =C-c S t= | Toggle Dirvish sidebar |

*** Dirvish Navigation

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =RET= | Open file/enter directory |
| =^= | Go up to parent directory |
| =TAB= | Toggle subtree expansion |
| =q= | Quit Dirvish |

*** Dirvish Modes

Press =C-c d m= in Dirvish to toggle between:
- *Pretty mode*: File sizes, timestamps, git status, subtree expansion
- *Ultra mode*: Minimal display for maximum speed

** Index Management

*** Rebuilding Indexes

Press =C-c S I= to rebuild all indexes:
- Recoll index (full-text) — runs as user process
- Plocate index (filenames) — requests systemd service restart (may require sudo)

*** Automatic Indexing

NixOS configures automatic indexing:
- Plocate: hourly via =services.locate=
- Recoll: every 4 hours via =systemd.timers.recoll-index=

*** Checking Status

Press =C-c S ?= to see:
- Which tools are available (✓ / ✗)
- Search capabilities per tool
- Supported file extensions
- Index database status

** Customization

Change the library path:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq seforim-directory \"~/path/to/your/seforim/\")
#+end_src

Change supported file extensions:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(setq seforim-file-extensions '(\"org\" \"pdf\" \"epub\" \"docx\" \"doc\" \"odt\" \"txt\"))
#+end_src

** Search Tools Comparison

| Tool | Type | Speed | Index Required | Searches Inside PDFs | Best For |
|------|------|-------|----------------|---------------------|----------|
| plocate | Filename | Instant | Yes | N/A | Finding files by name |
| fd | Filename | Fast | No | N/A | Fallback file find |
| recoll | Full-text | Instant | Yes | ✓ | Searching all content |
| ripgrep-all | Full-text | Fast | No | ✓ | Fallback content search |

* Programming Languages

** Supported Languages

| Language | Mode | LSP (Eglot) | Org Babel | Format on Save |
|----------|------|-------------|-----------|----------------|
| Python | python-mode | ✓ | ✓ | — |
| Rust | rust-mode | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Java | java-mode | ✓ (eglot-java) | — | — |
| Shell | sh-mode | — | ✓ | — |
| Emacs Lisp | emacs-lisp-mode | — | ✓ | — |
| Typst | typst-ts-mode | ✓ (tinymist) | — | — |

** LSP with Eglot

Eglot provides IDE features via Language Server Protocol:

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Completion | Context-aware suggestions (via Corfu) |
| Diagnostics | Real-time error checking |
| Hover | Documentation on hover |
| Go to definition | Jump to symbol definition |
| Find references | Find all uses of symbol |
| Rename | Rename symbol across project |

*** Eglot Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =M-.= | Go to definition |
| =M-,= | Go back |
| =M-?= | Find references |
| =C-c C-r= | Rename symbol |
| =C-h .= | Show documentation at point |

*** Starting Eglot

Eglot starts automatically for Python, Rust, Java, and Typst. To start manually:

: M-x eglot RET

*** Required Language Servers

All provided by =emacs.nix=:

| Language | Server | NixOS Package |
|----------|--------|---------------|
| Python | pylsp | =python3Packages.python-lsp-server= |
| Rust | rust-analyzer | =rust-analyzer= |
| Java | jdtls | =jdt-language-server= |
| Typst | tinymist | =tinymist= |

** Python

*** Running Python Code

In an Org file:

#+begin_example
,#+begin_src python
print(\"Hello, World!\")
x = 1 + 2
print(f\"Result: {x}\")
,#+end_src
#+end_example

Press =C-c C-c= to execute.

*** Python REPL

: M-x run-python RET

** Rust

*** Cargo Integration

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c C-c C-b= | cargo build |
| =C-c C-c C-r= | cargo run |
| =C-c C-c C-t= | cargo test |

*** Running Rust in Org

#+begin_example
,#+begin_src rust
fn main() {
    println!(\"Hello from Rust!\");
}
,#+end_src
#+end_example

Requires =ob-rust= (included in config).

** Java

Eglot-java provides:
- Project detection
- Compilation
- Test running
- Refactoring

Start with =M-x eglot= in a Java file.

** Shell Scripts

File extensions =.sh=, =.bash=, =.zsh= activate =sh-mode=.

Execute in Org:

#+begin_example
,#+begin_src shell
echo \"Current directory: $(pwd)\"
ls -la
,#+end_src
#+end_example

** Electric Pairs

Auto-closing brackets are enabled only in:
- Programming modes
- LaTeX, ConTeXt, Typst
- Org source block editing (=C-c '=)

Disabled in prose modes to avoid interfering with Hebrew writing.

** Rainbow Delimiters

Matching parentheses are color-coded in all programming modes for easier navigation of nested structures.

** Whitespace Display

In programming modes, trailing whitespace and tabs are highlighted.

** Completion with Corfu

Corfu provides inline completion in all modes:
- Auto-triggers after 2 characters (0.2s delay)
- Popup info shows documentation for selected candidate
- Cycle through candidates with =TAB=

* Embark

Embark provides contextual actions on targets (files, buffers, symbols, URLs, etc.).

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-.= | Embark act (show actions for target at point) |
| =C-;= | Embark dwim (do what I mean - default action) |
| =C-h B= | Embark bindings (show all bindings) |

** Using Embark

1. Place cursor on a target (filename, URL, symbol, etc.)
2. Press =C-.=
3. A menu appears with relevant actions
4. Press a key to execute action

** Example Actions

*** On a File Path

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =f= | Find file |
| =o= | Find file other window |
| =d= | Delete file |
| =r= | Rename file |
| =c= | Copy file |
| =w= | Copy path to kill ring |

*** On a URL

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =b= | Browse URL |
| =w= | Copy URL |
| =d= | Download |

*** On a Symbol

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =h= | Describe symbol |
| =.= | Go to definition |
| =r= | Find references |

*** On a Buffer

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =k= | Kill buffer |
| =r= | Rename buffer |
| =s= | Save buffer |

** Embark Collect

Press =E= during minibuffer completion to collect all candidates into a buffer for further action.

** Embark with Consult

When using consult commands (=C-s=, =C-x b=, etc.), press =C-.= on any candidate to act on it without selecting.

* File Management with Dirvish

Dirvish is a modern Dired enhancement providing a polished file management experience.

** Opening Dirvish

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-x C-j= | Jump to current file's directory |
| =C-x d= | Open Dirvish (dired) |

** Basic Navigation

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =RET= | Open file or enter directory |
| =^= | Go to parent directory |
| =q= | Quit Dirvish |
| =g= | Refresh |
| =j= | Jump to file (by name) |

** File Operations

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =C= | Copy file(s) |
| =R= | Rename/move file(s) |
| =D= | Delete file(s) |
| =+= | Create directory |
| =m= | Mark file |
| =u= | Unmark file |
| =U= | Unmark all |
| =t= | Toggle marks |

** Dirvish-Specific Features

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =TAB= | Toggle subtree (expand/collapse directory) |
| =C-c d m= | Toggle pretty/ultra mode |

** Display Modes

*** Pretty Mode (Default)

Shows:
- File sizes
- Modification times
- Git status indicators
- Subtree expansion
- Collapse indicators

*** Ultra Mode

Minimal display for:
- Maximum performance
- Large directories
- Slow filesystems

Toggle with =C-c d m= in Dirvish.

** Dirvish Sidebar

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c S t= | Toggle sidebar (in Seforim context) |

The sidebar provides a tree view alongside your editing buffer.

* Version Control with Magit

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-x g= | Magit status |
| =C-x M-g= | Magit dispatch (all commands) |
| =C-c g= | Magit file dispatch |
| =C-c G= | Git timemachine |

** Magit Status Buffer

Press =C-x g= to open the status buffer:

#+begin_example
Head:     main Fix typo in readme
Merge:    origin/main

Untracked files (2)
  new-file.org
  another-file.txt

Unstaged changes (1)
  modified   config.org

Staged changes (1)
  modified   init.el

Recent commits
  abc1234 Fix typo in readme
  def5678 Add new feature
#+end_example

** Basic Workflow

1. Open status: =C-x g=
2. Stage changes: =s= on files (or =S= for all)
3. Commit: =c c=, write message, =C-c C-c=
4. Push: =P p=

** Common Operations

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =s= | Stage file/hunk |
| =u= | Unstage file/hunk |
| =c c= | Commit |
| =P p= | Push |
| =F p= | Pull |
| =b b= | Switch branch |
| =b c= | Create branch |
| =l l= | Log |
| =d d= | Diff |
| =z z= | Stash |
| =z p= | Pop stash |

** Git Gutter

In programming modes, the gutter shows:
- Green: Added lines
- Red: Deleted lines
- Yellow: Modified lines

** Git Timemachine

Press =C-c G= to browse file history:

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| =p= | Previous version |
| =n= | Next version |
| =g= | Go to specific revision |
| =w= | Copy current version's hash |
| =q= | Quit timemachine |

* Adding New Features

** Adding a New Package

*** Method 1: Edit config.org (Recommended)

1. Open =~/.emacs.d/config.org=: =C-c e i=

2. Find appropriate section or create new heading

3. Add a =use-package= block:

#+begin_example
,** My New Package

,#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package package-name
  :ensure t
  :config
  ;; Configuration here
  (setq some-variable value))
,#+end_src
#+end_example

4. Save the file (auto-tangles to =config.el=)

5. Either:
   - Restart Emacs, OR
   - Place cursor in the block and press =C-c C-c= to evaluate

*** Method 2: Quick Install (Temporary)

1. =M-x package-install RET=
2. Type package name
3. Press =RET=

Note: This won't persist after restart unless added to =config.org=.

*** Method 3: Via NixOS (for packages needing system libraries)

For packages that need C libraries or system integration (like =jinx=, =pdf-tools=):

1. Add to =emacs.nix=:

#+begin_src nix
(epkgs.emacsWithPackages (p: [
  p.jinx
  p.exec-path-from-shell
  p.pdf-tools
  p.your-new-package  # ← Add here
]))
#+end_src

2. Rebuild: =sudo nixos-rebuild switch=

3. In =config.org=, use =:ensure nil=:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package your-new-package
  :ensure nil  ; Provided by Nix
  :config
  ...)
#+end_src

** Use-Package Syntax Reference

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package package-name
  :ensure t                      ; Install if not present (nil for Nix packages)
  :hook (mode-name . function)   ; Add to mode hook
  :bind ((\"C-c x\" . command))    ; Global keybinding
  :bind (:map mode-map           ; Mode-specific keybinding
         (\"C-c y\" . command))
  :custom                        ; Set custom variables
  (variable-name value)
  :init                          ; Run BEFORE package loads
  (some-setup-function)
  :config                        ; Run AFTER package loads
  (setq some-variable value)
  :after other-package           ; Load after dependency
  :defer t                       ; Lazy load
  :mode \"\\\\.ext\\\\'\"              ; Associate with file extension
  :diminish                      ; Hide from modeline
  :commands (cmd1 cmd2))         ; Autoload these commands
#+end_src

** Common Use-Package Patterns

*** Adding a Hook

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package flycheck
  :hook (prog-mode . flycheck-mode))
#+end_src

*** Adding Keybindings

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package avy
  :bind ((\"M-j\" . avy-goto-char-timer)
         (\"M-g l\" . avy-goto-line)))
#+end_src

*** Loading After Another Package

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package evil-collection
  :after evil
  :config
  (evil-collection-init))
#+end_src

*** Conditional Loading

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package pdf-tools
  :if (display-graphic-p)   ; Only in GUI
  :mode \"\\\\.pdf\\\\'\")
#+end_src

** Adding a New Programming Language

1. Find the major mode package (e.g., =go-mode= for Go)

2. Add to =config.org=:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package go-mode
  :mode \"\\\\.go\\\\'\"
  :hook (go-mode . eglot-ensure)
  :config
  (setq gofmt-command \"goimports\")
  (add-hook 'before-save-hook 'gofmt-before-save nil t))
#+end_src

3. Add LSP server to eglot (if needed):

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'eglot
  (add-to-list 'eglot-server-programs '(go-mode . (\"gopls\"))))
#+end_src

4. For Org Babel support, add:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(use-package ob-go
  :after org
  :config
  (org-babel-do-load-languages
   'org-babel-load-languages
   (append org-babel-load-languages '((go . t)))))
#+end_src

5. Install system dependencies (language, LSP server) via NixOS in =emacs.nix=.

** Adding a New Org Capture Template

In =config.org=, find the capture templates section and add:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'org
  (add-to-list 'org-capture-templates
               '(\"m\" \"Meeting\" entry
                 (file+headline \"~/Documents/org/meetings.org\" \"Meetings\")
                 \"* %^{Meeting Title}\\n  :PROPERTIES:\\n  :DATE: %U\\n  :END:\\n  %?\")))
#+end_src

** Adding a New Org-Roam Template

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'org-roam
  (add-to-list 'org-roam-capture-templates
               '(\"s\" \"sefer\" plain \"%?\"
                 :target (file+head \"%<%Y%m%d%H%M%S>-${slug}.org\"
                                   \"#+title: ${title}\\n#+filetags: sefer hebrew\\n\\n\")
                 :unnarrowed t)))
#+end_src

** Adding a Custom Function

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defun my/custom-function ()
  \"Description of what this function does.\"
  (interactive)  ; Makes it callable via M-x
  ;; Your code here
  (message \"Function executed!\"))

;; Bind to key
(global-set-key (kbd \"C-c x\") 'my/custom-function)
#+end_src

** Adding a New Hydra Menu

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defhydra my/hydra-example (:color blue :hint nil)
  \"
My Custom Menu

_a_: Action A    _b_: Action B
_c_: Action C    _q_: quit
\"
  (\"a\" some-function-a)
  (\"b\" some-function-b)
  (\"c\" some-function-c)
  (\"q\" nil))

(global-set-key (kbd \"C-c X\") 'my/hydra-example/body)
#+end_src

** Adding a New Snippet

1. Create snippet directory:

#+begin_src shell
mkdir -p ~/.emacs.d/snippets/MODENAME-mode/
#+end_src

2. Create snippet file (e.g., =~/.emacs.d/snippets/org-mode/hebrew-header=):

#+begin_example
# -*- mode: snippet -*-
# name: Hebrew Document Header
# key: hebhead
# --
#+TITLE: ${1:כותרת}
#+AUTHOR: שאול
#+DATE: `(format-time-string \"%Y-%m-%d\")`
#+LATEX_CLASS: article-unlimited

$0
#+end_example

3. Reload snippets: =M-x yas-reload-all=

4. Use: Type =hebhead= and press =TAB=

** Reloading Configuration

| Keybinding | Action |
|------------|--------|
| =C-c e i= | Open config file |
| =C-c e r= | Reload config |

Or manually:
1. Delete =~/.emacs.d/config.el=
2. Restart Emacs

