#+AUTHOR: User
#+STARTUP: overview num
#+OPTIONS: toc:3

* Installation
** NixOS Configuration

1. Copy =emacs-packages.nix= to =/etc/nixos/=

2. Edit =/etc/nixos/configuration.nix= and add:

   #+begin_src nix
     imports = [
       ./hardware-configuration.nix
       ./emacs-packages.nix
     ];
   #+end_src

3. Rebuild:

   #+begin_src shell
     sudo nixos-rebuild switch
   #+end_src

** Emacs Configuration

1. Create directory:

   #+begin_src shell
     mkdir -p ~/.emacs.d
   #+end_src

2. Copy files:

   #+begin_src shell
     cp config.org ~/.emacs.d/
     cp init.el ~/.emacs.d/
   #+end_src

3. Create required directories:

   #+begin_src shell
     mkdir -p ~/Documents/roam
     mkdir -p ~/Documents/org
     touch ~/Documents/bibliography.bib
   #+end_src

4. Start Emacs and wait for packages to install

** First Run Tasks

After first launch:

1. Install icon fonts:
   : M-x all-the-icons-install-fonts RET

2. Initialize org-roam database:
   : M-x org-roam-db-sync RET

* Basic Emacs Usage
** Key Notation

| Notation | Meaning              |
|----------+----------------------|
| =C-=     | Hold Control key     |
| =M-=     | Hold Alt/Meta key    |
| =S-=     | Hold Shift key       |
| =RET=    | Press Enter          |
| =SPC=    | Press Space          |

** Essential Commands

| Keybinding  | Description              |
|-------------+--------------------------|
| =C-x C-f=   | Open/create file         |
| =C-x C-s=   | Save file                |
| =C-x C-c=   | Quit Emacs               |
| =C-x b=     | Switch buffer            |
| =C-x k=     | Close buffer             |
| =C-g=       | Cancel command           |
| =M-x=       | Run any command          |
| =C-h k=     | Describe keybinding      |
| =C-h f=     | Describe function        |
| =C-h v=     | Describe variable        |

** Navigation

| Keybinding      | Description               |
|-----------------+---------------------------|
| =C-f= / =C-b=   | Forward/backward char     |
| =M-f= / =M-b=   | Forward/backward word     |
| =C-n= / =C-p=   | Next/previous line        |
| =C-a= / =C-e=   | Beginning/end of line     |
| =M-<= / =M->=   | Beginning/end of buffer   |
| =C-v= / =M-v=   | Page down/up              |
| =C-s=           | Search (consult-line)     |
| =M-g g=         | Go to line number         |

** Editing

| Keybinding | Description               |
|------------+---------------------------|
| =C-d=      | Delete character          |
| =M-d=      | Delete word               |
| =C-k=      | Kill to end of line       |
| =C-w=      | Kill region (cut)         |
| =M-w=      | Copy region               |
| =C-y=      | Yank (paste)              |
| =M-y=      | Cycle kill ring           |
| =C-/=      | Undo                      |
| =C-?=      | Redo                      |
| =C-x u=    | Visual undo tree          |

** Windows

| Keybinding | Description                     |
|------------+---------------------------------|
| =C-x 2=    | Split horizontally (and follow) |
| =C-x 3=    | Split vertically (and follow)   |
| =C-x 0=    | Close current window            |
| =C-x 1=    | Close other windows             |
| =M-o=      | Switch window (ace-window)      |

** Focused Writing Mode

| Keybinding | Action                           |
|------------+----------------------------------|
| =C-c w=    | Toggle focused writing (80-col centered) |
| =C-c W=    | Set column width                 |

When enabled, text is soft-wrapped at 80 columns and visually centered in the window. No actual line breaks are inserted.

* Hebrew and RTL Support
** How Direction Works

The config automatically sets direction per major mode:

| Mode Type              | Default Direction |
|------------------------+-------------------|
| Org, LaTeX, text modes | RTL (Hebrew)      |
| Programming modes      | LTR (English)     |
| Other modes            | Auto-detect       |

You can always override manually with the keybindings below.

** Switching Languages

| Keybinding | Action                                 |
|------------+----------------------------------------|
| =F9=       | Toggle input method (Hebrew ↔ English) |
| =C-c d=    | Toggle text direction (RTL ↔ LTR)      |
| =C-c r=    | Force RTL direction                    |
| =C-c l=    | Force LTR direction                    |
| =C-c D=    | Direction controls menu (hydra)        |

** Visual Indicators

The modeline shows current state:
- =[RTL עב]= - Hebrew mode, right-to-left
- =[LTR EN]= - English mode, left-to-right

** Best Practices

1. *Hebrew documents*: Automatically RTL (Org, LaTeX, text modes)
2. *English documents*: Use =C-c l= for LTR, or it auto-detects
3. *Mixed documents*: Use =C-c d= to toggle as needed
4. *Code*: Automatically LTR (prog-mode)

** Spell Checking

Spell checking uses Flyspell with Hunspell. Switch between languages manually:

| Keybinding | Action                      |
|------------+-----------------------------|
| =C-c s e=  | Switch to English           |
| =C-c s h=  | Switch to Hebrew            |
| =M-$=      | Correct word at point       |
| =i=        | Add word to personal dict   |

When a correctly-spelled word is flagged (e.g., Hebrew word while in English mode), press =i= to add it to your personal dictionary (=~/.hunspell_personal=).

Spell checking is active in:
- All text/prose modes (Org, LaTeX, Markdown, etc.)
- Comments and strings in programming modes

* Document Systems Overview

This configuration supports three document systems:

| System    | Best For                          | Hebrew | Nested Footnotes          |
|-----------+-----------------------------------+--------+---------------------------|
| *LaTeX*   | Academic papers, journals         | ✓      | Separate blocks (bigfoot) |
| *ConTeXt* | Complex typography, books         | ✓      | Native local footnotes    |
| *Typst*   | Fast iteration, modern syntax     | ✓      | Sequential (with show rule) |

** Quick Document Creation

| Keybinding | Action                    |
|------------+---------------------------|
| =C-c h l=  | Create Hebrew LaTeX doc   |
| =C-c h x=  | Create Hebrew ConTeXt doc |
| =C-c h t=  | Create Hebrew Typst doc   |
| =C-c h o=  | Create Hebrew Org doc     |

** Document Systems Menu

Press =C-c P= to open the document systems hydra menu with all preamble insertion options.

* Org Mode
** Basic Structure

#+begin_example
,* Heading Level 1
,** Heading Level 2
,*** Heading Level 3 (unlimited levels)
,**** Level 4
,***** Level 5
,****** Level 6 (and so on...)

Regular paragraph text.

- Unordered list item
  - Nested item (unlimited nesting)
    - Even more nested
      - And more...

1. Ordered list item
   1. Nested numbered item
      1. More nesting
         1. Unlimited levels
#+end_example

** Headings (Unlimited Levels)

| Syntax   | Level      |
|----------+------------|
| =*=      | Level 1    |
| =**=     | Level 2    |
| =***=    | Level 3    |
| =****=   | Level 4    |
| ...      | (no limit) |

** Heading Keybindings

| Keybinding      | Action                       |
|-----------------+------------------------------|
| =M-RET=         | Insert heading at same level |
| =M-S-RET=       | Insert TODO heading          |
| =M-LEFT/RIGHT=  | Promote/demote heading       |
| =M-UP/DOWN=     | Move heading up/down         |
| =TAB=           | Cycle visibility             |
| =S-TAB=         | Cycle global visibility      |

** Lists (Unlimited Levels)

*** Unordered Lists

#+begin_example
- Item 1
  - Sub-item (indent 2 spaces)
    - Sub-sub-item
      - And so on...
+ Alternative bullet
  + Sub-item
#+end_example

*** Ordered Lists

#+begin_example
1. First item
   1. Sub-item
      1. Sub-sub-item
         1. Unlimited nesting
2. Second item
#+end_example

*** List Keybindings

| Keybinding      | Action                 |
|-----------------+------------------------|
| =M-RET=         | Insert new list item   |
| =M-LEFT/RIGHT=  | Change indent level    |
| =M-UP/DOWN=     | Move item up/down      |
| =C-c -=         | Cycle bullet style     |
| =C-c C-c=       | Toggle checkbox        |

** Checkboxes

#+begin_example
- [ ] Unchecked item
- [X] Checked item
- [-] Partially done
#+end_example

** Links

#+begin_example
[[https://example.com][Link text]]
[[file:./other-file.org][Internal link]]
[[#custom-id][Link to heading]]
#+end_example

| Keybinding | Action           |
|------------+------------------|
| =C-c C-l=  | Insert/edit link |
| =C-c C-o=  | Open link        |

** Footnotes

#+begin_example
This text has a footnote[fn:1].

[fn:1] This is the footnote content.
#+end_example

| Keybinding  | Action                               |
|-------------+--------------------------------------|
| =C-c C-x f= | Insert footnote                      |
| =C-c C-c=   | Jump between footnote and reference  |
| =C-c h f=   | Fix RTL in all existing footnotes    |

*** Hebrew Footnote RTL Fix

Hebrew footnotes in Org mode can display as LTR because the footnote marker =[fn:N]= starts with LTR characters. The config automatically inserts a Right-to-Left Mark when creating new footnotes. For existing footnotes, use =C-c h f= to fix them all at once.

** Code Blocks

#+begin_example
,#+begin_src python
  print(\"Hello, World!\")
,#+end_src

,#+begin_src shell
  echo \"Shell command\"
,#+end_src
#+end_example

| Keybinding | Action                   |
|------------+--------------------------|
| =C-c C-c=  | Execute code block       |
| =C-c '=    | Edit in dedicated buffer |

Note: Electric pairs (auto-closing brackets) are active when editing code blocks via =C-c '=.

** Exporting

| Keybinding    | Action                 |
|---------------+------------------------|
| =C-c C-e=     | Open export dispatcher |
| =C-c C-e l l= | Export to LaTeX        |
| =C-c C-e l p= | Export to PDF          |
| =C-c C-e h h= | Export to HTML         |

** Org to LaTeX Export Settings

Add to the top of your org file:

#+begin_example
,#+TITLE: Document Title
,#+AUTHOR: Your Name
,#+LANGUAGE: he
,#+LATEX_COMPILER: lualatex
,#+LATEX_CLASS: article-unlimited
#+end_example

Available classes:
- =article-unlimited= (default)
- =report-unlimited=
- =book-unlimited=

* LaTeX and AUCTeX
** Creating a New Document

1. Create file: =C-x C-f myfile.tex RET=
2. AUCTeX mode activates automatically

Or use: =C-c h l= to create a Hebrew LaTeX document with full preamble.

** Basic Document Structure (Hebrew)

#+begin_src latex
  \\documentclass{article}
  \\usepackage{fontspec}
  \\usepackage[bidi=basic]{babel}
  \\babelprovide[main, import]{hebrew}
  \\babelprovide[import]{english}
  \\babelfont{rm}{David CLM}

  \\begin{document}

  \\section{כותרת}

  טקסט בעברית.

  \\begin{english}
  English text here.
  \\end{english}

  \\end{document}
#+end_src

** Quick Preamble Insertion

| Command                              | Action                         |
|--------------------------------------+--------------------------------|
| =M-x my/insert-hebrew-preamble=      | Insert Babel Hebrew preamble   |
| =M-x my/insert-polyglossia-preamble= | Insert Polyglossia preamble    |
| =M-x my/insert-unlimited-structure=  | Insert only unlimited structure|

Or use =C-c P= for the document systems menu.

** AUCTeX Keybindings

| Keybinding    | Action                      |
|---------------+-----------------------------|
| =C-c C-c=     | Run command (compile, view) |
| =C-c C-a=     | Compile and view            |
| =C-c C-v=     | View PDF                    |
| =C-c C-e=     | Insert environment          |
| =C-c C-s=     | Insert section              |
| =C-c C-m=     | Insert macro                |
| =C-c C-f C-b= | Bold                        |
| =C-c C-f C-i= | Italic                      |
| =C-c `=       | Go to next error            |

** Compiling Documents

1. Press =C-c C-c=
2. Press =RET= for default (LaTeX)
3. View with =C-c C-c= then =View=

Documents compile with LuaLaTeX automatically.

** Unlimited Sectioning in LaTeX

| Level | Command                       |
|-------+-------------------------------|
| 1     | =\\section{}=                  |
| 2     | =\\subsection{}=               |
| 3     | =\\subsubsection{}=            |
| 4     | =\\paragraph{}=                |
| 5     | =\\subparagraph{}=             |
| 6     | =\\subsubparagraph{}=          |
| 7     | =\\subsubsubparagraph{}=       |
| 8     | =\\subsubsubsubparagraph{}=    |
| 9     | =\\subsubsubsubsubparagraph{}= |
| 10    | (continue pattern)            |

** Unlimited Lists in LaTeX

Standard LaTeX limits lists to 4 levels. With our config, you can nest up to 20 levels:

#+begin_src latex
  \\begin{itemize}
    \\item Level 1
    \\begin{itemize}
      \\item Level 2
      \\begin{itemize}
        \\item Level 3
        % ... continue to level 20
      \\end{itemize}
    \\end{itemize}
  \\end{itemize}
#+end_src

* Nested Footnotes in LaTeX
** Overview

The =bigfoot= package provides multiple footnote levels. Each level appears in its own *separate block* at the bottom of the page.

** Method 1: Simple Footnotes (Non-Nested)

Use =\\footnoteB{}=, =\\footnoteC{}=, etc. directly from body text. Each level gets its own block:

#+begin_src latex
  Main text.\\footnote{First level footnote.}

  More text.\\footnoteB{Second level, appears in separate block.}
#+end_src

*Result at page bottom:*
#+begin_example
  ─────────────────────────────
  ¹ First level footnote.

  ─────────────────────────────
  ¹ Second level, appears in separate block.
#+end_example

** Method 2: True Nesting (Footnote Within Footnote)

To place a footnote *inside* another footnote, use =\\footnotemarkX= + =\\footnotetextX{}=:

#+begin_src latex
  \\usepackage{bigfoot}
  \\DeclareNewFootnote{default}
  \\DeclareNewFootnote{B}
  \\DeclareNewFootnote{C}

  \\begin{document}

  Main text.\\footnote{%
    This is the first-level footnote. It discusses a concept\\footnotemarkB{}
    that needs further explanation.
    \\footnotetextB{%
      This second-level footnote appears in its own separate block
      at the bottom of the page, below the first-level footnotes.
      It can even contain another level\\footnotemarkC{}.
      \\footnotetextC{Third level footnote, in its own block.}%
    }%
  }

  \\end{document}
#+end_src

*Result at page bottom:*
#+begin_example
  ─────────────────────────────
  ¹ This is the first-level footnote. It discusses a concept¹
    that needs further explanation.

  ─────────────────────────────
  ¹ This second-level footnote appears in its own separate block
    at the bottom of the page, below the first-level footnotes.
    It can even contain another level¹.

  ─────────────────────────────
  ¹ Third level footnote, in its own block.
#+end_example

** Footnote Levels Reference

| Command          | Level | Use                      |
|------------------+-------+--------------------------|
| =\\footnote{}=    | 1     | Normal footnotes         |
| =\\footnoteB{}=   | 2     | Second level             |
| =\\footnoteC{}=   | 3     | Third level              |
| =\\footnoteD{}=   | 4     | Fourth level             |
| =\\footnoteE{}=   | 5     | Fifth level              |
| =\\footnoteF{}=   | 6     | Sixth level              |
| =\\footnoteG{}=   | 7     | Seventh level            |
| =\\footnoteH{}=   | 8     | Eighth level             |
| =\\footnoteI{}=   | 9     | Ninth level              |
| =\\footnoteJ{}=   | 10    | Tenth level              |

For nesting, use:
- =\\footnotemarkB= through =\\footnotemarkJ= (places the superscript)
- =\\footnotetextB{}= through =\\footnotetextJ{}= (places the text in its block)

** Keybindings

*** Simple Footnotes (=C-c f <number>=)

| Keybinding | Action                   |
|------------+--------------------------|
| =C-c f 1=  | Insert =\\footnote{}=     |
| =C-c f 2=  | Insert =\\footnoteB{}=    |
| =C-c f 3=  | Insert =\\footnoteC{}=    |
| =C-c f 4=  | Insert =\\footnoteD{}=    |
| =C-c f 5=  | Insert =\\footnoteE{}=    |
| =C-c f 6=  | Insert =\\footnoteF{}=    |
| =C-c f 7=  | Insert =\\footnoteG{}=    |
| =C-c f 8=  | Insert =\\footnoteH{}=    |
| =C-c f 9=  | Insert =\\footnoteI{}=    |
| =C-c f 0=  | Insert =\\footnoteJ{}=    |

*** Nested Footnotes (via Hydra Menu)

Press =C-c F= to open the footnotes hydra, then:

| Key | Action                    |
|-----+---------------------------|
| =m= | → Footnote marks sub-menu |
| =t= | → Footnote texts sub-menu |
| =n= | Insert nested template    |

In the *marks* sub-menu:

| Key | Action                  |
|-----+-------------------------|
| =2= | Insert =\\footnotemarkB= |
| =3= | Insert =\\footnotemarkC= |
| =4= | Insert =\\footnotemarkD= |
| =5= | Insert =\\footnotemarkE= |
| =b= | ← Back to main menu     |

In the *texts* sub-menu:

| Key | Action                    |
|-----+---------------------------|
| =2= | Insert =\\footnotetextB{}= |
| =3= | Insert =\\footnotetextC{}= |
| =4= | Insert =\\footnotetextD{}= |
| =5= | Insert =\\footnotetextE{}= |
| =b= | ← Back to main menu       |

** Adding More Levels

If you need more than 10 levels, add to your preamble:

#+begin_src latex
  \\DeclareNewFootnote{K}
  \\DeclareNewFootnote{L}
#+end_src

Then use =\\footnoteK{}=, =\\footnotemarkK=, =\\footnotetextK{}=, etc.

* ConTeXt
** What is ConTeXt?

ConTeXt is a TeX-based document system with:
- Native RTL/Hebrew support (no packages needed)
- Built-in local footnotes (nested footnotes in separate blocks)
- Excellent typography
- \"Batteries included\" philosophy

** Creating a ConTeXt Document

Use =C-c h x= to create a Hebrew ConTeXt document, or create manually:

#+begin_src tex
  \\mainlanguage[he]
  \\setupalign[r2l]
  \\definefontfamily[hebrew][rm][David CLM]
  \\definefontfamily[hebrew][ss][Nachlieli CLM]
  \\definefontfamily[hebrew][tt][Miriam Mono CLM]
  \\setupbodyfont[hebrew]

  \\starttext

  \\startsection[title={מבוא}]

  טקסט בעברית.

  \\stopsection

  \\stoptext
#+end_src

** Compiling ConTeXt

In Emacs:
1. Press =C-c C-c=
2. Type =ConTeXt= and press =RET=

Or from command line:

#+begin_src shell
  context myfile.tex
#+end_src

** ConTeXt Sectioning

| Command                            | Description   |
|------------------------------------+---------------|
| =\\startpart[title={...}]=          | Part          |
| =\\startchapter[title={...}]=       | Chapter       |
| =\\startsection[title={...}]=       | Section       |
| =\\startsubsection[title={...}]=    | Subsection    |
| =\\startsubsubsection[title={...}]= | Subsubsection |

Each must be closed with =\\stop...=

** Local Footnotes in ConTeXt

ConTeXt's killer feature: footnotes that appear at the bottom of their *parent footnote*, not at the bottom of the page.

#+begin_src tex
  זהו טקסט ראשי.\\footnote{
    זוהי הערת שוליים ראשונה.
    \\startlocalfootnotes
    מונח כלשהו.\\localfootnote{
      הערה מקוננת — מופיעה בתחתית ההערה הראשונה, לא בתחתית העמוד.
      \\startlocalfootnotes
      עוד מונח.\\localfootnote{
        הערה שלישית — בתחתית ההערה השנייה.
      }
      \\placelocalfootnotes
      \\stoplocalfootnotes
    }
    \\placelocalfootnotes
    \\stoplocalfootnotes
  }
#+end_src

*Result:*
- Level 1 footnote → bottom of page
- Level 2 footnote → bottom of level 1 block
- Level 3 footnote → bottom of level 2 block

Use =C-c P= then =f= to insert a local footnote structure.

* Typst
** What is Typst?

Typst is a modern document system with:
- Instant compilation (watch mode)
- Clean, readable syntax
- Native RTL support
- Easy scripting
- ~50MB vs ~4GB for LaTeX

** Creating a Typst Document

Use =C-c h t= to create a Hebrew Typst document, or create manually:

#+begin_src typst
  #set text(lang: \"he\", font: \"David CLM\")
  #set page(flipped: true)
  #set heading(numbering: \"1.1.1\")

  = מבוא

  טקסט בעברית.

  == תת-כותרת

  עוד טקסט.
#+end_src

** Compiling Typst

| Keybinding | Action                              |
|------------+-------------------------------------|
| =C-c C-c=  | Compile once                        |
| =C-c C-w=  | Watch mode (live preview)           |
| =C-c C-v=  | View PDF                            |
| =C-c t h=  | Insert Hebrew preamble              |
| =C-c t f=  | Insert nested footnote helper       |

Or from command line:

#+begin_src shell
  typst compile myfile.typ      # Compile once
  typst watch myfile.typ        # Watch mode
#+end_src

** Typst Syntax Basics

*** Headings

#+begin_src typst
  = Level 1
  == Level 2
  === Level 3
  ==== Level 4 (unlimited)
#+end_src

*** Text Formatting

#+begin_src typst
  *bold*
  _italic_
  `code`
  #underline[underlined]
  #strike[strikethrough]
#+end_src

*** Lists

#+begin_src typst
  - Unordered item
    - Nested item
      - More nesting (unlimited)

  + Ordered item
    + Nested ordered
#+end_src

*** Links

#+begin_src typst
  #link(\"https://example.com\")[Link text]
#+end_src

** Basic Footnotes in Typst

#+begin_src typst
  This is text#footnote[This is a footnote].
#+end_src

** Nested Footnotes in Typst

*** The Problem

By default, Typst doesn't allow you to place a =#footnote= inside another =#footnote=. If you try, you'll get an error because Typst tries to render all footnote entries inline in a single flow at the bottom of the page.

*** The Solution

Add this show rule at the top of your document:

#+begin_src typst
  #show footnote.entry: it => {
    block(it)
  }
#+end_src

*** Why It Works

By wrapping each =footnote.entry= in a =block()=:

1. *Isolates each footnote entry* into its own independent layout container
2. *Breaks the layout dependency* between parent and child footnotes
3. Each footnote can now independently place its own sub-footnotes

*** Full Working Example

#+begin_src typst
  // This is the critical line: wrap each footnote entry in its own block
  #show footnote.entry: it => {
    block(it)
  }

  This is body text with a footnote.#footnote[
    This is the first-level footnote, and it contains
    another footnote inside it.#footnote[
      This is a second-level (nested) footnote! You can even
      go deeper.#footnote[
        Third level nested footnote.
      ]
    ]
  ]

  More body text here.#footnote[A normal, non-nested footnote for comparison.]
#+end_src

*Result at page bottom:*

#+begin_example
  ──────────────────────────────────────
  ¹ This is the first-level footnote, and it contains
    another footnote inside it.²
  ² This is a second-level (nested) footnote! You can even
    go deeper.³
  ³ Third level nested footnote.
  ⁴ A normal, non-nested footnote for comparison.
#+end_example

Each footnote gets its own numbered entry, laid out sequentially at the page bottom.

*** Hebrew Example

#+begin_src typst
  #set text(lang: \"he\", font: \"David CLM\")
  #set page(flipped: true)

  // Enable nested footnotes
  #show footnote.entry: it => {
    block(it)
  }

  = מבוא

  זהו טקסט ראשי עם הערת שוליים.#footnote[
    זוהי הערת שוליים ראשונה, המכילה הערה נוספת.#footnote[
      זוהי הערת שוליים מקוננת! אפשר להמשיך לעומק.#footnote[
        הערת שוליים ברמה שלישית.
      ]
    ]
  ]
#+end_src

*** Optional Styling

You can add styling while keeping the block wrapper:

#+begin_src typst
  #show footnote.entry: it => {
    block({
      set text(size: 9pt)
      it
    })
  }
#+end_src

*** Caveats

- *Numbering is flat*: All footnotes share one sequential counter (1, 2, 3...). Typst doesn't distinguish \"levels.\"
- *All appear at page bottom*: Nested footnotes don't appear indented under their parent — they're sequential entries.
- *Deep nesting*: 2–3 levels work well; extremely deep nesting may be confusing.

*** Comparison with LaTeX/ConTeXt

| Feature              | LaTeX (bigfoot)     | ConTeXt              | Typst                |
|----------------------+---------------------+----------------------+----------------------|
| Separate blocks      | ✓ Each level        | ✓ Hierarchical       | ✗ Sequential         |
| Numbering            | Per-level           | Per-level            | Flat (shared)        |
| True hierarchy       | ✓                   | ✓                    | ✗                    |
| Ease of use          | Medium              | Easy                 | Easy                 |
| Setup required       | Package + declare   | None                 | One show rule        |

* Nested Footnotes Comparison

| Feature                    | LaTeX (bigfoot)       | ConTeXt               | Typst                  |
|----------------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+------------------------|
| *Setup required*           | Package + declare     | None (built-in)       | One show rule          |
| *Syntax*                   | =\\footnotemarkB= etc. | =\\localfootnote{}=    | =#footnote[]= inside   |
| *Separate visual blocks*   | ✓ Yes                 | ✓ Yes (hierarchical)  | ✗ No (sequential)      |
| *Numbering*                | Per-level             | Per-level             | Flat (all share)       |
| *Max levels*               | Unlimited             | Unlimited             | ~3 practical           |
| *Best for*                 | Journals, academic    | Books, complex docs   | Quick iteration        |

*Recommendations:*
- For true hierarchical footnotes in separate blocks: *ConTeXt*
- For separate blocks with journal compatibility: *LaTeX* with bigfoot
- For fast iteration with acceptable nesting: *Typst*

* Org-Roam
** What is Org-Roam?

Org-roam is a note-taking system based on the Zettelkasten method. It creates a network of interconnected notes.

** Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action              |
|------------+---------------------|
| =C-c n f=  | Find or create node |
| =C-c n i=  | Insert link to node |
| =C-c n l=  | Toggle backlinks    |
| =C-c n c=  | Capture new node    |
| =C-c n g=  | Show graph          |
| =C-c n t=  | Add tag             |
| =C-c n r=  | Add reference       |
| =C-c n a=  | Add alias           |
| =C-c n d=  | Go to today's daily |
| =C-c n D=  | Go to specific date |
| =C-c n h=  | Find Hebrew nodes   |
| =C-c n R=  | Find research nodes |
| =C-c n x=  | Open random node    |
| =C-c R=    | Org-roam hydra menu |

** Creating Notes

1. Press =C-c n f=
2. Type a title
3. If it doesn't exist, press =RET= to create
4. Choose a template:
   - =d= Default
   - =h= Hebrew
   - =r= Research
   - =c= Concept
   - =l= Literature note
   - =p= Project
5. Write your note
6. Save with =C-x C-s=

** Linking Notes

1. In a note, press =C-c n i=
2. Search for note to link to
3. Select and press =RET=

** Viewing Backlinks

Press =C-c n l= to see all notes that link TO the current note.

** Visual Graph

Run =M-x org-roam-ui-open= to see your notes as an interactive graph.

** Daily Notes

| Keybinding | Action                |
|------------+-----------------------|
| =C-c n d=  | Go to today's daily   |
| =C-c n D=  | Go to a specific date |

* Bibliography
** Citar Keybindings

| Keybinding | Action               |
|------------+----------------------|
| =C-c b i=  | Insert citation      |
| =C-c b o=  | Open reference       |
| =C-c b n=  | Open notes           |
| =C-c b r=  | Refresh bibliography |

** Bibliography File

Location: =~/Documents/bibliography.bib=

** Adding References

1. Open =~/Documents/bibliography.bib=
2. Add entries in BibTeX format:

#+begin_src bibtex
  @article{smith2024,
    author = {Smith, John},
    title = {Article Title},
    journal = {Journal Name},
    year = {2024},
    volume = {1},
    pages = {1--10}
  }

  @book{jones2023,
    author = {Jones, Sarah},
    title = {Book Title},
    publisher = {Publisher Name},
    year = {2023}
  }
#+end_src

3. Save the file
4. Run =C-c b r= to refresh

** Using in LaTeX

#+begin_src latex
  \\usepackage[backend=biber]{biblatex}
  \\addbibresource{~/Documents/bibliography.bib}

  % In document:
  \\cite{smith2024}

  % At end:
  \\printbibliography
#+end_src

** Using in Org

#+begin_example
See [cite:@smith2024] for details.
#+end_example

